  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts bone-cell redox balance in younger adults (about 20–30 years) with older adults (over 55), showing how ageing shifts reactive oxygen species, or ROS, from a controlled signal to a damaging force. In both panels, mitochondria and NOX contribute to ROS production, while antioxidant defenses such as SOD, GPx, GSH, CAT, and FOXO counteract them. p66Shc is shown feeding mitochondrial ROS generation, and sex steroids appear to suppress ROS.\n\nIn the younger state, antioxidant systems and sex steroids keep ROS in check, so oxidative stress is controlled. This supports regulated apoptosis and autophagy, leading to balanced bone remodelling, normal bone mineral density and microarchitecture, and low fracture risk.\n\nWith ageing, the diagram shows stronger ROS output and weaker restraint, despite the same antioxidant components being present. The result is oxidative stress, macromolecular damage, and cell senescence. Apoptosis becomes uncontrolled, with autophagy still present but apparently insufficient. The downstream consequences are increased osteoblast and osteocyte apoptosis, microdamage or “microptrosis” as labeled, unbalanced bone remodelling, reduced bone mineral density, deteriorated bone microarchitecture, and a higher fracture risk. Overall, the image links age-related oxidative stress to skeletal fragility.",
